\description{
Compare sparse single-response, independent multichannel, joint multinomial
and right-censored Cox PH models. Select methods by a common information
criterion or observation-row cross-validation, collect elapsed timings and
extract a comparison table with \code{\link[=l0glmMetrics]{l0glmMetrics()}}.
}
\details{
L0glm uses \code{\link[=l0glmTune]{l0glmTune()}} for its automatic full-data grid. LASSO variants
use \code{glmnet}; ABESS searches support sizes; L0Learn searches L0 penalties.
MCP/SCAD use optional \code{ordinis} for Gaussian/logit/Poisson-log models and
optional \code{ncvreg} for Cox. Original dimensions and scales are retained.
Backends unable to constrain a nonnegative Gaussian/Poisson intercept
report this limitation; L0glm and L0Learn support that bound.

Adaptive weights come from exactly one
\code{l0glmFit(start=NULL, lambda="gic", maxit=1, grouped=FALSE, thresh=0)} call. Independent-channel weights are
\eqn{\max(|\widetilde\beta_{jk}|,\epsilon)^{-\gamma}}. Grouped and
multinomial glmnet paths use pilot row RMS instead of signed averages;
multinomial factors are shared across categories even for ungrouped fits.
Intercepts are unpenalized. Unscaled penalty factors and ridge coefficients
are retained; glmnet rescales its factors internally. The pilot and entire
sparse/refitting path are recomputed using training data in each CV fold.
Relaxed variants use a fully relaxed support-restricted refit (\code{gamma=0}),
without tuning a mixture of penalized and unpenalized coefficients.

CV minimizes original-family held-out deviance per total validation weight,
or per weighted trial for multinomial. Cox uses Breslow partial-likelihood
subtraction with the full risk sets, per held-out event, as in \code{\link[=l0glmTune]{l0glmTune()}}.
Folds share observation rows across channels, and whole multinomial count
rows remain together. Fold means and SE use the conventions of \code{\link[=l0glmTune]{l0glmTune()}}.
Screening and ISIS selection are recomputed within each training fold.
Excluded predictors return zero coefficients in the original dimensions.
IC scores retain the original number of predictors after screening.
Per-method \code{screening} diagnostics and training-fold diagnostics are retained
in \code{tuning}; elapsed timings include screening and every ISIS candidate fit.
L0glm sum-loss penalties are scaled by the training likelihood-mass fraction;
other backend lambdas retain their native units. The selected model uses all
data. CV used to select a model is not an independent test of that model.

Multinomial comparisons support L0glm, ABESS and all four glmnet variants.
ABESS counts use at most one weighted row per observation/category, without
expanding individual trials. Reference coefficients are reconstructed and
all outputs are centered to CLR. Multinomial MCP, SCAD and L0Learn are
unsupported. Cox supports L0glm, ABESS, glmnet variants and MCP/SCAD via
ncvreg. ABESS and ncvreg require untied times and unit weights for a common
Breslow comparison. ncvreg also requires zero offsets and unrestricted
coefficients. Different tie conventions are not silently
mixed. L0Learn has no Cox loss. For glmnet, zero times are replaced by
order-preserving positive ranks, leaving Cox risk sets and ties unchanged.
\subsection{Native file-backed LASSO}{

\code{method="biglasso"} uses the optional \code{biglasso} package for Gaussian identity,
binary logistic, grouped multiresponse Gaussian or Breslow Cox LASSO paths.
It requires unrestricted coefficients, a free GLM intercept and weights of
zero or one. Gaussian offsets are subtracted before fitting; other offsets
are unsupported. Independent Gaussian/logistic channels are also available.
The shared likelihood code selects lambda by the requested IC or CV.

A complete double \code{bigmemory} design is passed directly to the native solver.
Training and CV row views reuse that backing matrix through \code{row.idx};
validation rows are omitted from fitting and standardization. No full dense
copy is made. Predictors must be finite, without missing values on this
native path. Other source formats, column subsets or mean imputation use
bounded working matrices, usually after SIS/ISIS. Large sparse designs also
require screening before conversion to biglasso's dense storage. Resident
inputs are copied to a double \code{big.matrix} once and reused across CV folds.

Use \code{screen="none"} for plain LASSO on all predictors, retaining biglasso's
own convex-path screening rules. Shared SIS/ISIS otherwise follows the same
rules as the other methods. Set \code{options=list(biglasso=list(ncores=8L))} for
eight native threads; the default is one. \code{native.screen} selects biglasso's
internal rule without changing the shared \code{screen} argument. Timings include
input conversion when needed, path fitting, common scoring and CV.

\code{seconds} includes the full-data path, adaptive pilot, scoring and CV;
\code{path_seconds} and \code{cv_seconds} separate these components. Missing packages,
unsupported combinations and failures retain explanatory status rows so
remaining methods can finish. L0Learn's six-digit lambda reporting is
accounted for when matching training-fold paths to the requested grid.
}
}
\examples{
# Sparse Gaussian regression.
set.seed(1)
X <- matrix(rnorm(360),60,6)
truth <- c(2,-1,rep(0,4))
y <- as.numeric(X \%*\% truth + rnorm(60))
comparison <- sparseGLM(X,y,method=c("lasso","relaxed_lasso"),
  tune="gic",nlambda=5,beta_true=truth)
l0glmMetrics(comparison)[c("method","seconds","gic","adj_R2_weighted","F1_score","MCC")]
plot(comparison,which=c(1,2))

# The same SIS/ISIS controls apply to every supported method. Each ISIS round
# uses that method's own selected model to screen for additional predictors.
set.seed(2)
wide <- matrix(rnorm(100*2000),100,2000)
truth_wide <- c(3,-2,1.5,rep(0,1997))
y_wide <- as.numeric(wide\%*\%truth_wide)+rnorm(100)
screened <- sparseGLM(wide,y_wide,method=c("L0glm","lasso","relaxed_lasso"),
  screen="ISIS",nsis=10,nlambda=5,beta_true=truth_wide,
  lambda=list(L0glm=presetLambda("gic",ncol(wide),nrow(wide))),
  options=list(L0glm=list()))
l0glmMetrics(screened)[c("method","screen","seconds","gic","TP","FP","F1_score","MCC")]
# With tune="cv", all screening and adaptive pilots use training rows only.
screened_cv <- sparseGLM(wide,y_wide,method="lasso",screen="SIS",nsis=20,
  tune="cv",nfolds=3,nlambda=5,beta_true=truth_wide)
l0glmMetrics(screened_cv)[c("method","screen","seconds","TP","FP","F1_score","MCC")]

# Blurred Poisson spike trains: smaller versions of the single/multichannel
# deconvolution benchmarks. MCP and SCAD are omitted because they were slow
# on the large deconvolution examples. All times below are measured locally.
comparison_methods <- c("L0glm","abess","L0Learn","lasso","relaxed_lasso",
                        "adaptive_lasso","adaptive_relaxed_lasso")
fields <- c("method","seconds","gic","deviance","adj_R2_weighted","F1_score","MCC")

# File-backed plain LASSO, with a free intercept and BIC or CV selection.
if (requireNamespace("biglasso", quietly=TRUE) &&
    requireNamespace("bigmemory", quietly=TRUE)) {
  set.seed(7)
  A <- matrix(rnorm(100*500),100,500)
  y <- 2*A[,1]-2*A[,2]+rnorm(100)
  truth <- c(2,-2,rep(0,498))
  source <- l0glmData(bigmemory::as.big.matrix(A))
  comparison <- sparseGLM(source,y,method="biglasso",screen="none",
    tune="bic",nlambda=10L,options=list(ncores=2L),beta_true=truth)
  l0glmMetrics(comparison)[c("method","seconds","gic","TP","FP")]
  cv <- sparseGLM(source,y,method="biglasso",screen="none",
    tune="cv",nfolds=3L,nlambda=10L)
  predict(cv,source[1:3,,drop=FALSE])
}

# Single channel: 50 spikes among 500 candidate locations.
single <- simul(method=2,n=500,p=500,s=.1,seed=2)
lambda_gic <- log(ncol(single$X))*log(log(nrow(single$X)))
comparison_single <- sparseGLM(single,family=poisson(identity),
  method=comparison_methods,tune="gic",nlambda=10,
  nonnegative=TRUE,intercept=FALSE,approximation="wls",
  support.size=1:100,lambda=list(L0glm=lambda_gic),
  options=list(L0glm=list(),abess=list(warm.start=TRUE)))
l0glmMetrics(comparison_single)[fields]
plot(comparison_single,which=c(1,2,4,5))

# Multichannel: use 100 time points and six abundant mass channels for speed.
multi <- simul(method=4,n=100,p=100,s=.1,seed=2)
keep <- head(order(Matrix::colSums(multi$y),decreasing=TRUE),6)
for (name in c("y","y_true","beta_true")) multi[[name]] <- multi[[name]][,keep,drop=FALSE]
lambda_gic <- log(ncol(multi$X))*log(log(nrow(multi$X)))
comparison_multi <- sparseGLM(multi,family=poisson(identity),
  method=comparison_methods,tune="gic",nlambda=10,
  nonnegative=TRUE,intercept=FALSE,approximation="wls_grouped",
  support.size=1:20,lambda=list(L0glm=lambda_gic),
  options=list(L0glm=list(),abess=list(warm.start=TRUE)))
# ABESS and glmnet select rows jointly using Gaussian shared-row-weight WLS;
# L0glm fits the exact grouped Poisson model. L0Learn fits channels independently.
# GIC, deviance and prediction R-squared always use the original Poisson model.
l0glmMetrics(comparison_multi)[c(fields,"within_row_F1_score","within_row_MCC")]

plot(comparison_multi,which=c(1,4),type="coefs",channels="all")
plot(comparison_multi,which=c(1,2,4,5),channels="mean")
# One ground-truth/estimate pair; heatmaps default to which=1.
plot(comparison_multi,type="heatmap")
# Two pages, each with its own ground-truth/estimate pair.
plot(comparison_multi,type="heatmap",which=c(1,2))
}
